{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-40516" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Find $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)$, where $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071032/state.json" "file_sha256" "65f1c822d09041b9cc0874dfae740192bea0805206adfa450f1680fea9293707"} {"question" "q-40516" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe linear transformation $Q$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $Q$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of ${R}^{n}$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $||Q(x)||=||x||.$\n\n- If $x⊥y$ then $Q(x)⊥Q(y)$ for any $x,y\\in {R}^{n}.$ In general, $Q$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $Q$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nIn this case, since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x)\\cdot Q(y) & =x\\cdot y \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-2)+(-1)\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071032/state.json" "file_sha256" "65f1c822d09041b9cc0874dfae740192bea0805206adfa450f1680fea9293707"} {"question" "q-40516" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071032/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fbf632517b5aacad38c953e53eb8705d463a1cd471ddee89b71db001b1340c6d"} {"question" "q-40516" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071032/state.json" "file_sha256" "65f1c822d09041b9cc0874dfae740192bea0805206adfa450f1680fea9293707"} {"question" "q-40516" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-3"] ["math" "-4"] ["math" "-5"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "6"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071032/state.json" "file_sha256" "65f1c822d09041b9cc0874dfae740192bea0805206adfa450f1680fea9293707"} {"question" "q-40516" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-5"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071032/state.json" "file_sha256" "65f1c822d09041b9cc0874dfae740192bea0805206adfa450f1680fea9293707"} {"question" "q-177950" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Find $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)$, where $x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071032/state.json" "file_sha256" "65f1c822d09041b9cc0874dfae740192bea0805206adfa450f1680fea9293707"} {"question" "q-177950" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe linear transformation $Q$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $Q$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of ${R}^{n}$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $||Q(x)||=||x||.$\n\n- If $x⊥y$ then $Q(x)⊥Q(y)$ for any $x,y\\in {R}^{n}.$ In general, $Q$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $Q$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nIn this case, since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x)\\cdot Q(y) & =x\\cdot y \\\\  & =0\\cdot (-7)+1\\cdot (-5)+1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071032/state.json" "file_sha256" "65f1c822d09041b9cc0874dfae740192bea0805206adfa450f1680fea9293707"} {"question" "q-177950" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071032/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fbf632517b5aacad38c953e53eb8705d463a1cd471ddee89b71db001b1340c6d"} {"question" "q-177950" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071032/state.json" "file_sha256" "65f1c822d09041b9cc0874dfae740192bea0805206adfa450f1680fea9293707"} {"question" "q-177950" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-4"] ["math" "-8"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "8"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071032/state.json" "file_sha256" "65f1c822d09041b9cc0874dfae740192bea0805206adfa450f1680fea9293707"} {"question" "q-177950" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-4"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071032/state.json" "file_sha256" "65f1c822d09041b9cc0874dfae740192bea0805206adfa450f1680fea9293707"} {"question" "q-177769" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Find $‖Q(x)‖$, where $x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ \\sqrt{6}\\end{aligned}].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071032/state.json" "file_sha256" "65f1c822d09041b9cc0874dfae740192bea0805206adfa450f1680fea9293707"} {"question" "q-177769" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe linear transformation $Q$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $Q$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of ${R}^{n}$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $||Q(x)||=||x||.$\n\n- If $x⊥y$ then $Q(x)⊥Q(y)$ for any $x,y\\in {R}^{n}.$ In general, $Q$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $Q$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nIn this case, since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Q(x)‖ & =‖x‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{3}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{6})}^{2}} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071032/state.json" "file_sha256" "65f1c822d09041b9cc0874dfae740192bea0805206adfa450f1680fea9293707"} {"question" "q-177769" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071032/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fbf632517b5aacad38c953e53eb8705d463a1cd471ddee89b71db001b1340c6d"} {"question" "q-177769" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071032/state.json" "file_sha256" "65f1c822d09041b9cc08