Q(y) & =x\\cdot y \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-4)+(-1)\\cdot 4+2\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071032/state.json" "file_sha256" "65f1c822d09041b9cc0874dfae740192bea0805206adfa450f1680fea9293707"} {"question" "q-40513" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071032/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fbf632517b5aacad38c953e53eb8705d463a1cd471ddee89b71db001b1340c6d"} {"question" "q-40513" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071032/state.json" "file_sha256" "65f1c822d09041b9cc0874dfae740192bea0805206adfa450f1680fea9293707"} {"question" "q-40513" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-3"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "10"] ["math" "6"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071032/state.json" "file_sha256" "65f1c822d09041b9cc0874dfae740192bea0805206adfa450f1680fea9293707"} {"question" "q-40513" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071032/state.json" "file_sha256" "65f1c822d09041b9cc0874dfae740192bea0805206adfa450f1680fea9293707"} {"question" "q-177810" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Find $‖Q(x)‖$, where $x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071032/state.json" "file_sha256" "65f1c822d09041b9cc0874dfae740192bea0805206adfa450f1680fea9293707"} {"question" "q-177810" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe linear transformation $Q$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $Q$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of ${R}^{n}$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $||Q(x)||=||x||.$\n\n- If $x⊥y$ then $Q(x)⊥Q(y)$ for any $x,y\\in {R}^{n}.$ In general, $Q$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $Q$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nIn this case, since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Q(x)‖ & =‖x‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{(-2\\sqrt{3})}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071032/state.json" "file_sha256" "65f1c822d09041b9cc0874dfae740192bea0805206adfa450f1680fea9293707"} {"question" "q-177810" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071032/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fbf632517b5aacad38c953e53eb8705d463a1cd471ddee89b71db001b1340c6d"} {"question" "q-177810" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071032/state.json" "file_sha256" "65f1c822d09041b9cc0874dfae740192bea0805206adfa450f1680fea9293707"} {"question" "q-177810" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\sqrt{3}"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "5\\sqrt{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071032/state.json" "file_sha256" "65f1c822d09041b9cc0874dfae740192bea0805206adfa450f1680fea9293707"} {"question" "q-177810" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071032/state.json" "file_sha256" "65f1c822d09041b9cc0874dfae740192bea0805206adfa450f1680fea9293707"} {"question" "q-177075" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{u}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}],\\,{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors above, and let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0$\n- ${Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})}$ is orthogonal\n- ${Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})}$ is orthonormal" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071032/state.json" "file_sha256" "65f1c822d09041b9cc0874dfae740192bea0805206adfa450f1680fea9293707"} {"question" "q-177075" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe linear transformation $Q$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $Q$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of ${R}^{n}$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $||Q(x)||=||x||.$\n\n- If $x⊥y$ then $Q(x)⊥Q(y)$ for any $x,y\\in {R}^{n}.$ In general, $Q$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $Q$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]=18-18=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $Q({u}_{1})⊥Q({u}_{2})$ since\n\n$$\nQ({u}_{1})\\cdot Q({u}_{2})={u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that, for example, $Q({u}_{1})$ is not a unit vector since\n\n$$\n‖Q({u}_{1})‖=‖{u}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{{9}^{2}+{6}^{2}}\\ne 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071032/state.json" "file_sha256" "65f1c822d09041b9cc0874dfae740192bea0805206adfa450f1680fea9293707"} {"question" "q-177075" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071032/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fbf632517b5aacad38c953e53eb8705d463a1cd471ddee89b71db001b1340c6d"} {"question" "q-177075" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071032/state.json" "file_sha256" "65f1c822d09041b9cc0874dfae740192bea0805206adfa450f1680fea9293707"} {"question" "q-177075" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "None of the statements are true."]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071032/state.json" "file_sha256" "65f1c822d09041b9cc0874dfae740192bea0805206adfa450f1680fea9293707"} {"question" "q-177075" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071032/state.json" "file_sha256" "65f1c822d09041b9cc0874dfae740192bea0805206adfa450f1680fea9293707"} {"question" "q-178026" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{u}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}],\\,{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors above, and let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0$\n- ${Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})}$ is orthogonal\n- ${Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})}$ is orthonormal" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071032/state.json" "file_sha256" "65f1c822d09041b9cc0874dfae740192bea0805206adfa450f1680fea9293707"} {"question" "q-178026" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe linear transformation $Q$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $Q$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of ${R}^{n}$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $||Q(x)||=||x||.$\n\n- If $x⊥y$ then $Q(x)⊥Q(y)$ for any $x,y\\in {R}^{n}.$ In general, $Q$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $Q$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}]=-\\frac{12}{25}+\\frac{12}{25}=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $Q({u}_{1})⊥Q({u}_{2})$ since\n\n$$\nQ({u}_{1})\\cdot Q({u}_{2})={u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, both $Q({u}_{1})$ and $Q({u}_{2})$ are unit vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Q({u}_{1})‖ & =‖{u}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{(\\frac{4}{5})^{2}+(\\frac{3}{5})^{2}}=1 \\\\ ‖Q({u}_{2})‖ & =‖{u}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{(-\\frac{3}{5})^{2}+(\\frac{4}{5})^{2}}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071032/state.json" "file_sha256" "65f1c822d09041b9cc0874dfae740192bea0805206adfa450f1680fea9293707"} {"question" "q-178026" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071032/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fbf632517b5aacad38c953e53eb8705d463a1cd471ddee89b71db001b1340c6d"} {"question" "q-178026" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071032/state.json" "file_sha256" "65f1c822d09041b9cc0874dfae740192bea0805206adfa450f1680fea9293707"} {"question" "q-178026" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "None of the statements are true."]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071032/state.json" "file_sha256" "65f1c822d09041b9cc0874dfae740192bea0805206adfa450f1680fea9293707"} {"question" "q-178026" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I, II, and III"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071032/state.json" "file_sha256" "65f1c822d09041b9cc0874dfae740192bea0805206adfa450f1680fea9293707"} {"question" "q-177118" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{u}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{u}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors above, and let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0$\n- ${Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2}),Q({u}_{3})}$ is orthogonal\n- ${Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2}),Q({u}_{3})}$ is orthonormal" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071032/state.json" "file_sha256" "65f1c822d09041b9cc0874dfae740192bea0805206adfa450f1680fea9293707"} {"question" "q-177118" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false since\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=2+12-5=9\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that, for example, $Q({u}_{1})⊥̸Q({u}_{2})$ since\n\n$$\nQ({u}_{1})\\cdot Q({u}_{2})={u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false since the set is not even orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071032/state.json" "file_sha256" "65f1c822d09041b9cc0874dfae740192bea0805206adfa450f1680fea9293707"} {"question" "q-177118" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071032/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fbf632517b5aacad38c953e53eb8705d463a1cd471ddee89b71db001b1340c6d"} {"question" "q-177118" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071032/state.json" "file_sha256" "65f1c822d09041b9cc0874dfae740192bea0805206adfa450f1680fea9293707"} {"question" "q-177118" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "None of the statements are true."]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071032/state.json" "file_sha256" "65f1c822d09041b9cc0874dfae740192bea0805206adfa450f1680fea9293707"} {"question" "q-177118" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "None of the statements are true."] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071032/state.json" "file_sha256" "65f1c822d09041b9cc0874dfae740192bea0805206adfa450f1680fea9293707"} {"question" "q-177315" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{u}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}],\\,{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors above, and let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0$\n- ${Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})}$ is orthogonal\n- ${Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})}$ is orthonormal" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071032/state.json" "file_sha256" "65f1c822d09041b9cc0874dfae740192bea0805206adfa450f1680fea9293707"} {"question" "q-177315" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe linear transformation $Q$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $Q$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of ${R}^{n}$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $||Q(x)||=||x||.$\n\n- If $x⊥y$ then $Q(x)⊥Q(y)$ for any $x,y\\in {R}^{n}.$ In general, $Q$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $Q$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]=-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $Q({u}_{1})⊥Q({u}_{2})$ since\n\n$$\nQ({u}_{1})\\cdot Q({u}_{2})={u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, both $Q({u}_{1})$ and $Q({u}_{2})$ are unit vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Q({u}_{1})‖ & =‖{u}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})^{2}+(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})^{2}}=1 \\\\ ‖Q({u}_{2})‖ & =‖{u}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})^{2}+(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})^{2}}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071032/state.json" "file_sha256" "65f1c822d09041b9cc0874dfae740192bea0805206adfa450f1680fea9293707"} {"question" "q-177315" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071032/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fbf632517b5aacad38c953e53eb8705d463a1cd471ddee89b71db001b1340c6d"} {"question" "q-177315" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "valu