ix $Q$ of transformation $Q$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of ${R}^{n}$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $||Q(x)||=||x||.$\n\n- If $x⊥y$ then $Q(x)⊥Q(y)$ for any $x,y\\in {R}^{n}.$ In general, $Q$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $Q$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed,\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=6+3\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that, $Q({u}_{1})⊥̸Q({u}_{2})$ since\n\n$$\nQ({u}_{1})\\cdot Q({u}_{2})={u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false since the set is not even orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071032/state.json" "file_sha256" "65f1c822d09041b9cc0874dfae740192bea0805206adfa450f1680fea9293707"} {"question" "q-178269" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071032/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fbf632517b5aacad38c953e53eb8705d463a1cd471ddee89b71db001b1340c6d"} {"question" "q-178269" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071032/state.json" "file_sha256" "65f1c822d09041b9cc0874dfae740192bea0805206adfa450f1680fea9293707"} {"question" "q-178269" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None of the statements are true."]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071032/state.json" "file_sha256" "65f1c822d09041b9cc0874dfae740192bea0805206adfa450f1680fea9293707"} {"question" "q-178269" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "None of the statements are true."] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071032/state.json" "file_sha256" "65f1c822d09041b9cc0874dfae740192bea0805206adfa450f1680fea9293707"} {"question" "e-12954" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $\\mathbf{Q}$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. If ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071032/assets/86bb7df4c111a6fd93c1891d3fceafdbcbe2c49dd80a9e91f623f7e97120d7d0.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071032/assets/ac195edae526d86fdfdab05c1b118b14bd1756397027bec2ad360e0491d987e0.png), find\n\n- $\\Vert \\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{x}) \\Vert.$\n\n- $\\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{x}) \\cdot \\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{y}).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071032/example-12954.json" "file_sha256" "074b50daad888cb9222484e4ccdadbb53f181627f980f663c4e4131984ca0193"} {"question" "e-12954" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $\\mathbf{Q}$ is orthogonal, we have that $\\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{x}) \\cdot \\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{y}) = \\mathbf{x} \\cdot \\mathbf{y}$ for any vectors $\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\mathbf{y}.$\n\nThe linear transformation $\\mathbf{Q}$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $\\mathbf{Q}$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $|| \\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{x}) || = || \\mathbf{x} ||.$\n\n- If $\\mathbf{x} \\perp \\mathbf{y}$ then $\\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{x}) \\perp \\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{y})$ for any $\\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n.$ In general, $\\mathbf{Q}$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $\\mathbf{Q}$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nIn this case, since $\\mathbf{Q}$ is orthogonal, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Q(x)‖ & =‖x‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, $\\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{x}) \\cdot \\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{y}) = \\mathbf{x} \\cdot \\mathbf{y}.$ As a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x)\\cdot Q(y) & =x\\cdot y \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 3+(-2)\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071032/example-12954.json" "file_sha256" "074b50daad888cb9222484e4ccdadbb53f181627f980f663c4e4131984ca0193"} {"question" "e-12951" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071032/assets/aec65d50659286de9cbf0e03639e0dd1cbaa24307339398d47a825deed89a8d9.png)\n\nConsider the vectors above, and let $\\mathbf{Q}$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\mathbf{u}_1 \\cdot \\mathbf{u}_2 = 0$\n\n- $\\{\\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{u}_1),\\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{u}_2)\\}$ is orthogonal\n\n- $\\{\\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{u}_1),\\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{u}_2)\\}$ is orthonormal" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071032/example-12951.json" "file_sha256" "2d8fa97d1a730b64e0db61b866e9cd5d8b6a3090dc6c2b5bd33f1af33b4d0941"} {"question" "e-12951" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $\\mathbf{Q}$ is orthogonal, we have that $\\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{x}) \\cdot \\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{y}) = \\mathbf{x} \\cdot \\mathbf{y}$ for any vectors $\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\mathbf{y}.$\n\nThe linear transformation $\\mathbf{Q}$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $\\mathbf{Q}$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $|| \\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{x}) || = || \\mathbf{x} ||.$\n\n- If $\\mathbf{x} \\perp \\mathbf{y}$ then $\\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{x}) \\perp \\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{y})$ for any $\\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n.$ In general, $\\mathbf{Q}$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $\\mathbf{Q}$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed,\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071032/assets/bfc573de171024e15387f4c3b67d68d7423860c150f4453938e535a0ad05352f.png)\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $\\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{u}_1) \\perp \\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{u}_2)$ since\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{u}_1)\\cdot\\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{u}_2) = \\mathbf{u}_1\\cdot\\mathbf{u}_2 = 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, both $\\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{u}_1)$ and $\\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{u}_2)$ are unit vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Q({u}_{1})‖ & =‖{u}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{(\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3})^{2}+(\\frac{1}{3})^{2}}=1 \\\\ ‖Q({u}_{2})‖ & =‖{u}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{(-\\frac{1}{3})^{2}+(\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3})^{2}}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071032/example-12951.json" "file_sha256" "2d8fa97d1a730b64e0db61b866e9cd5d8b6a3090dc6c2b5bd33f1af33b4d0941"}] "usage" {"e-12954" [] "q-40516" [] "q-177315" [] "e-12951" [] "q-177075" []} "basis" 2737 "retractions" [[17592186198906 :question/problem "Let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Find $Q(x) \\cdot Q(y)$, where $x = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $y = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$."] [17592186198676 :question/problem "$u_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}, u_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the vectors above, and let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $u_{1} \\cdot u_{2} = 0$\n2. ${Q(u_{1}), Q(u_{2})}$ is orthogonal\n3. ${Q(u_{1}), Q(u_{2})}$ is orthonormal"] [17592186198677 :question/problem "$u_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}, u_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the vectors above, and let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $u_{1} \\cdot u_{2} = 0$\n2. ${Q(u_{1}), Q(u_{2})}$ is orthogonal\n3. ${Q(u_{1}), Q(u_{2})}$ is orthonormal"] [17592186198059 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{Q}$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. If $\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$, find\n\n1. $\\Vert \\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{x}) \\Vert$.\n2. $\\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{x}) \\cdot \\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{y})$."] [17592186198059 :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathbf{Q}$ is orthogonal, we have that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{x}) \\cdot \\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{y}) = \\mathbf{x} \\cdot \\mathbf{y}\n$$\n\nfor any vectors $\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\mathbf{y}$.\n\nThe linear transformation $\\mathbf{Q}$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $\\mathbf{Q}$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ is orthogonal.\n- $\\mid \\mid \\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{x}) \\mid \\mid = \\mid \\mid \\mathbf{x} \\mid \\mid$.\n- If $\\mathbf{x} \\perp \\mathbf{y}$ then $\\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{x}) \\perp \\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{y})$ for any $\\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$. In general, $\\mathbf{Q}$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n- $\\mathbf{Q}$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nIn this case, since $\\mathbf{Q}$ is orthogonal, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n‖Q(x)‖ &= ‖x‖ \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{x_{1}^{2} + x_{2}^{2} + x_{3}^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(-1)^{2} + (-2)^{2} + 2^{2}} \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso,\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{x}) \\cdot \\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{y}) = \\mathbf{x} \\cdot \\mathbf{y}\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ(x) \\cdot Q(y) &= x \\cdot y \\\\\n&= (-1) \\cdot 3 + (-2) \\cdot 0 + 2 \\cdot 5 \\\\\n&= 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186198058 :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the vectors above, and let $\\mathbf{Q}$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\mathbf{u}_1 \\cdot \\mathbf{u}_2 = 0$\n2. $\\{\\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{u}_1),\\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{u}_2)\\}$ is orthogonal\n3. $\\{\\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{u}_1),\\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{u}_2)\\}$ is orthonormal"] [17592186198058 :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathbf{Q}$ is orthogonal, we have that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{x}) \\cdot \\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{y}) = \\mathbf{x} \\cdot \\mathbf{y}\n$$\n\nfor any vectors $\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\mathbf{y}$.\n\nThe linear transformation $\\mathbf{Q}$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $\\mathbf{Q}$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ is orthogonal.\n- $\\mid \\mid \\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{x}) \\mid \\mid = \\mid \\mid \\mathbf{x} \\mid \\mid$.\n- If $\\mathbf{x} \\perp \\mathbf{y}$ then $\\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{x}) \\perp \\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{y})$ for any $\\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$. In general, $\\mathbf{Q}$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n- $\\mathbf{Q}$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed,\n$\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}$\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $\\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{u}_1) \\perp \\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{u}_2)$ since\n$\\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{u}_1)\\cdot\\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{u}_2) = \\mathbf{u}_1\\cdot\\mathbf{u}_2 = 0$.\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, both $\\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{u}_1)$ and $\\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{u}_2)$ are unit vectors:\n$‖Q(u_{1})‖|= ‖u_{1}‖ = \\sqrt{(\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3})^{2} + (\\frac{1}{3})^{2}} = 1; ‖Q(u_{2})‖|= ‖u_{2}‖ = \\sqrt{(-\\frac{1}{3})^{2} + (\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3})^{2}} = 1$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""]] "immutable_answers" [17592186194972 17592186194973 17592186194974 17592186194975 17592186194976 17592186194978 17592186194979 17592186194980 17592186194981 17592186194982 17592186197672 17592186197673 17592186197674 17592186197675 17592186197676] "immutable_fields" [[17592186194971 :answer-field/correct] [17592186194971 :answer-field/id] [17592186194971 :answer-field/key] [17592186194971 :answer-field/type] [17592186194977 :answer-field/correct] [17592186194977 :answer-field/id] [17592186194977 :answer-field/key] [17592186194977 :answer-field/type] [17592186197671 :answer-field/correct] [17592186197671 :answer-field/id] [17592186197671 :answer-field/key] [17592186197671 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
